WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.860
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome to the program
today, Henry Giroux, who is the chaired professor

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.210
of scholarship in the public interest at McMaster
University.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:14.700
His most recent book is Race, Politics and
Pandemic Pedagogy, published this year by

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.780
Bloomsbury.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:16.780
It's so great to have you on.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.000
I appreciate your time today.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.920
Speaker 4: Oh, I'm delighted to be here.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:26.119
Speaker 1: So, you know, there's been many
political analyzes made of this authoritarian

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:32.610
slide that we've seen that we could say led
up to the election of Donald Trump in 2016,

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.399
but then also was further fomented by the
election of Donald Trump in 2016.

00:00:37.399 --> 00:00:44.090
I'm interested to hear from you more, your
thoughts on the cultural aspect of this and

00:00:44.090 --> 00:00:50.000
how far back you think the the kind of catalysts
for this slide go and how we should sort of

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:51.399
be conceiving of it.

00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:57.879
Speaker 4: I mean, I think we've seen political
and cultural extremism in the United States.

00:00:57.879 --> 00:00:59.660
It has a long history.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.199
I mean, we've seen it with the McCarthy period.

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:03.340
We saw with the Red Scare.

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.939
And then, of course, there's the legacy of
colonization.

00:01:05.939 --> 00:01:10.450
I mean, there's the wiping out of indigenous
populations.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:12.340
There's Jim Crow, there's slavery.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.079
And I think that one of the things that this
country has failed to confront is not only

00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:22.039
that legacy, but how it tends to reappear
in different forms in different historical

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:23.039
moments.

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:27.829
And I think that where I see an enormous turning
point in the United States is in the 1970s,

00:01:27.829 --> 00:01:33.630
all of a sudden, it seems to me there's there's
a counter-revolution emerging against the

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:40.020
60s, against the democratization of the social
order, against the attempt to in some way

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:42.909
eliminate massive inequalities in wealth and
power.

00:01:42.909 --> 00:01:45.229
And with Reagan, something happens.

00:01:45.229 --> 00:01:49.149
And what happens is there's an enormous shift
in cultural values.

00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:54.159
And that shift moves away from supporting
the public good to basically, in many ways,

00:01:54.159 --> 00:01:58.569
destroying it and claiming that the market
now should be the template not simply for

00:01:58.569 --> 00:02:03.059
judging the economy or managing the economy,
but for all of social life.

00:02:03.059 --> 00:02:09.399
This has an enormously destructive consequence,
it seems to me, in the United States.

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:15.260
And it begins to give rise to far right wing
movements, who, for the most part, hate the

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.870
public good, hate the common good, and all
of a sudden find themselves on the side of

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:25.370
reproducing echoes of history that are pretty
dangerous, whether we're talking about right

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.430
wing extremism, racial purity, misogyny, militarism,
ultra nationalism.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:37.180
I mean, all these forces begin to come in
to become very visible and come to the surface

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:44.269
in light of the increasing misery that I call
neo liberalism or gangsta capitalism produces.

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:48.170
As a result of the 1980s,
Speaker 1: when I when I spoke to Noam Chomsky

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:53.799
a couple of months ago, the political points
that he thought were relevant in that period

00:02:53.799 --> 00:03:01.079
you're talking about include as a reaction
to the civil rights movement, the established

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:07.840
the establishment of the war on drugs, for
example, the fight against Roe v. Wade, and

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:14.030
that some of these points led to what the
Republican Party became in the early 90s under

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:15.030
Newt Gingrich.

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:18.080
And then we can sort of like follow the political
line.

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:23.390
Culturally, though, what was it about those
things that were happening prior to during

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:27.609
and after the 70s that kind of changed this
American right wing?

00:03:27.609 --> 00:03:31.140
Speaker 4: I mean, what you begin to see culturally,
a couple of things.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:40.090
Ideologically, you see the ideology of self-interest
reproduced in many ways and celebrity culture

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.290
and consumerism.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.959
Citizenship takes on a very different form
culturally after the 1980s.

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:50.349
All of a sudden, the obligations of citizenship
citizenship have to do with consumerism.

00:03:50.349 --> 00:03:54.901
The more becomes the temple of the state,
so to speak.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.219
But you also see something else.

00:03:56.219 --> 00:04:01.900
You see a rise of what I call the punishing
state and a decline of the social state.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:07.569
And with that comes a number of cultural apparatuses
that begin to reinforce what I call the most

00:04:07.569 --> 00:04:11.620
insidious cultural aspect of neo liberalism
or gangster capitalism.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.999
And that is the individualization of problems.

00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:16.769
Everything becomes privatized.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:19.799
All problems are a matter of individual responsibility.

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:24.530
And what that means culturally is it becomes
increasingly difficult for people to translate

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.000
private troubles into larger systemic considerations.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.090
So the social begins to collapse.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.220
Now, what happens as a result of that?

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:35.610
Culturally, what happens is you have the war
on drugs.

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.590
You have, of course, Clinton's war in the
welfare state.

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:41.090
You have the Southern strategy.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:47.170
I mean, all of things, all of these things
combine a question of racial purity and what

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:51.199
I call white supremacy with the logic of capitalism.

00:04:51.199 --> 00:04:57.430
Somebody has to be blamed and who has to be
blamed by immigrants or blacks or young people.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.990
You know, all those groups that are relative.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.660
Powerless in the face of this overwhelmingly
political and cultural apparatus.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:07.830
Now, David, there's something else, and I
think the other side of this is what we have

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:11.300
to understand is that education takes a radical
turn.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:17.940
In the 1980s, it increasingly becomes privatized,
commodified, undermined, defunded.

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.169
And all of a sudden, we're not talking about
educating people to be critical, engaged citizens

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:23.169
anymore.

00:05:23.169 --> 00:05:24.169
We're talking about them.

00:05:24.169 --> 00:05:26.229
We're talking about teachers to teach for
the test.

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:27.229
Yep.

00:05:27.229 --> 00:05:28.229
Yes.

00:05:28.229 --> 00:05:29.509
Runs through a whole range of presidents.

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:35.199
I mean, the Republican and the Democratic
parties both share a notion of education,

00:05:35.199 --> 00:05:42.449
is anti intellectual, destroys the civic imagination
and imposes what I call a pedagogy of repression

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:48.800
and basically produce increases of anti intellectualism
that is in the schools and in the larger cultural

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:49.800
apparatus.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:50.800
Right.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.069
Second point, don't confuse schooling with
education.

00:05:53.069 --> 00:05:54.479
That's it.

00:05:54.479 --> 00:05:58.220
Schooling is an institution that we know of
public higher, but education takes place in

00:05:58.220 --> 00:05:59.770
other spheres.

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:06.789
The new social media, the digital media, the
rise of what I call an Iraqi image based culture.

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:09.409
And these are largely controlled by the right.

00:06:09.409 --> 00:06:16.560
And so you have this tsunami of anti intellectualism,
right wing nonsense, lies, misinformation,

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.090
conspiracy theories emerging since the 1970s.

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.909
And you find their end point and Trump.

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:27.680
Speaker 1: Well, I want to get back to the
education piece because I have so many notes

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:28.680
about it.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:33.000
One small thing that I was thinking of in
your cultural idea of the kind of individualization

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:39.220
of problems, is this related to the gun issue
in the United States in this specific sense

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:46.680
that unlike in many other countries, too many
people in the U.S. see guns as a way to solve

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.750
problems that are that are really systemic
problems.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.410
But they say I will solve individually this
problem with a firearm.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:58.720
Speaker 4: I think it's a brilliant analysis
and I think it's right on target.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:03.069
I think that basically when you individualize
problems, you say that people have to take

00:07:03.069 --> 00:07:08.650
both responsible for those problems individually
and have the right to engage them in ways

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:12.289
that suggest that the government is the enemy
of freedom rather than something that could

00:07:12.289 --> 00:07:14.490
be useful in dealing with these issues.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:20.949
The other side of this is that when you individualize
problems in this way, you can't address them

00:07:20.949 --> 00:07:21.949
systemically.

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:25.030
So you begin to increasingly criminalize social
problems.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:29.820
So, you know, if you, for instance, acting
out in schools, you know, the principal doesn't

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.750
come down and say, hey, come on, let's talk.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:36.750
You call the police and all of a sudden you
have little five year olds in handcuffs in

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:37.750
kindergarten.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:43.099
So it seems to me with this increasing criminalization
of social problems, you have a notion of freedom

00:07:43.099 --> 00:07:48.900
emerging that is completely removed from any
notion of social responsibility, religious

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.160
freedom that simply says I can do what I want.

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:53.960
Self-interest is all that matters.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:55.330
All problems are individualized.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:59.270
This is what I call the individualization
of the social and the social sphere and the

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.710
social contract and the social good and the
public good.

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:04.990
David, begin to collapse.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:09.770
We lose those sites where people can understand
the interconnections that make a democracy

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:10.770
possible.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:16.400
Couple that with an education and schooling
system that basically produces new forms of

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.130
civic illiteracy.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:22.000
And you don't have a democracy anymore because
you can't have a democracy without informed

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.000
citizens.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.930
Speaker 1: So on education, I frequently will
get calls from viewers.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:31.960
I mean, listen, talking about individualizing
things, I will get calls from viewers who

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:40.610
say, my cousin, my uncle, whoever, they've
fallen for false beliefs about vaccines.

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:44.310
They've fallen for false beliefs about it
doesn't matter what.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:45.580
But they're not thinking.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.030
They've just they've fallen for it.

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:49.110
How do I talk them out of it?

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:53.850
And I struggle to say anything other than,
you know, at the individual level, it can

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.600
take weeks or months of really getting someone
to reexamine their beliefs.

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:00.210
That's like a retail strategy.

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:01.910
It's not a wholesale strategy.

00:09:01.910 --> 00:09:05.020
My instinct is it's too late.

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:10.500
And and really, if people learned critical
thinking and media literacy and epistemology

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:15.460
in middle school, we prevent this from even
happening in the first place to some degree,

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:19.280
not with everybody, but maybe we give people
the tools to not fall into this in the first

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:20.280
place.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.840
Is that a fair critique of of at least a piece
of what's wrong with the educational or schooling

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.220
system, as you call it today?

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:29.380
Speaker 4: No, I think it's an important piece.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.340
I mean, it's foundational and that the way
we educate people says something about the

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:36.790
kind of agents we produce and the kind of
future we want.

00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:41.000
And it seems to me education and schooling
in that sense is very long term.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.360
I think the other side of this is that, you
know, how do we talk about taking control

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:49.370
of these cultural apparatuses that basically
this imagination machines?

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:53.320
I mean, we're not just talking about how do
you say it?

00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:56.910
The ability to get people to think critically
or rationally or to be able to engage are

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:58.800
the positions that's important.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:09:59.800
But.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.260
We're also talking about larger systemic apparatuses
that need to be challenged by people like

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:10.000
us in social movements and reproduced in ways
that give people the opportunity to be able

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.850
to recognize that the most fundamental questions
are not whether they're falling for lies,

00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:16.930
but what kind of future do they want for their
children?

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:18.520
Do they want health care that's free?

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:22.130
Do they want to be in debt for the rest of
their lives?

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:25.540
Critical consciousness matters, but it has
to translate.

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.420
Ideas have to translate and be wedded to power.

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:32.321
And it seems to be when we removed the question
of power from the question of insight, we

00:10:32.321 --> 00:10:36.900
fall into a trap that we unconsciously reproduce
that we may want to stay away from.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.700
And that is to say, we'll just talk to each
other.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:40.700
No, I'm sorry.

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:42.680
It's not about just right.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:46.130
It's about changing the very institutions
that have put us there in the first place.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.960
And that's not just a burden of people who
are confused.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:53.450
That's that's a political obligation that
we all have to engage in with those people,

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:54.450
hopefully.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:59.130
Speaker 1: So in that sense, I mean, when
when you hear these very superficial narratives

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:06.910
of part of the problem is left and right,
don't engage anymore or the dialog has coarsened

00:11:06.910 --> 00:11:10.761
this type of stuff, is does that not really
strike you as particularly important?

00:11:10.761 --> 00:11:11.761
Speaker 4: That's a diversion.

00:11:11.761 --> 00:11:16.720
And I find that as a political diversion because
it doesn't deal with fundamental questions

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.490
like, look, what's the result of massive inequality
in the United States and where does it come

00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:22.490
from?

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:23.490
Right.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:26.290
Is it what does it mean when some people are
trading medicine for for for, you know, for

00:11:26.290 --> 00:11:27.290
food stamps?

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:30.160
You know, what does it mean when schools are
collapsing?

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.350
You know, that's not a matter of whether the
right and left can show up to each other.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:37.830
That's a matter of how do we talk about democracy
as a country if we're going to take democracy

00:11:37.830 --> 00:11:38.830
seriously?

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:40.130
And what are the forces that are undermining?

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.600
These are very different kinds of questions.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.820
What does it mean to live in a country where
economic activity is removed from social cost?

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.510
What's the consequence of that and how does
it bring it down on our lives?

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:57.490
The last thing is that we have to be able
to shame is a terrible pedagogical tool.

00:11:57.490 --> 00:12:01.310
I think shaming people does nothing but just
reinforce the worst divisions.

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:06.720
And I think the real question here for all
of us is how do we begin to use a language

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:11.960
in which people can recognize themselves,
in which we can shift consciousness, in ways

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:16.430
in which people can say, yeah, that's that
makes sense to me without putting their identities

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.620
on trial.

00:12:17.620 --> 00:12:21.710
We need a language rigorous, accessible in
order to make something meaningful, to make

00:12:21.710 --> 00:12:23.300
it critical, to make a transformative.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:28.100
Speaker 1: Can you give an example of that
in terms of on any issue, could you give an

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:32.650
example of the wrong way that the language
is being used and then how it could be improved?

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:38.040
Speaker 4: I mean, I think in talking to people
that, for instance, dealing with schools because

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.670
the schools are failing, it's terrible.

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:42.700
I think we should homeschool.

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:46.800
And one of the questions I always raise is
that if school is so crucial, do you think

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:51.480
school is crucial to democracy, to your life,
for your children?

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:52.480
Yes.

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:55.080
Then why is it being defunded?

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.090
Schools aren't failing, they're being defunded.

00:12:57.090 --> 00:13:00.500
I mean, the problem is not that schools are
failing.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:04.340
The problem is that they actually serve a
very important purpose in society.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:09.060
We have to ask ourselves, why do people want
to destroy them and why do some people have

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:10.720
access that you don't?

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.500
What can we do to make schools matter in your
community?

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:15.370
How would we do that together?

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.150
How would we speak to that?

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:18.390
How would we make schools more?

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.410
Richard, what resources do we need?

00:13:20.410 --> 00:13:21.410
What policies?

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:22.930
What does it mean to get on a school?

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:27.320
What can we do that in order to change a curriculum
so that you have, for instance, free meals

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:29.200
for your kid in the morning?

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:30.640
Kids starving can't learn.

00:13:30.640 --> 00:13:31.960
Sorry, you know, right.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:34.670
About 47 kids in a class can't learn.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:40.130
So I think what we start to deal with particulars
and make them resonate with the very conditions

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.400
that people could recognize in their lives.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.080
We make something meaningful to make it.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:45.990
Well, I'll give you one last example.

00:13:45.990 --> 00:13:48.940
People often say to me, why don't you come
to the United States, come back?

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:51.170
And I say, well, would you get national health
care?

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:56.160
And so what do you mean, I say, look, I've
had two knee surgeries I haven't paid.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:57.510
I don't pay a penny.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:00.980
A cent, you know, my drugs are basically free.

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:07.080
I can walk into a corner doctor's office,
a drop in center and get free medical care.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:08.770
And they don't believe this stuff.

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:13.050
And, you know, when I say, you know, most
debt in the United States is now due to medical

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:14.050
expenses.

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:15.050
Right.

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:18.540
I mean, you know what I know just recently,
one hospital, I think, in Tennessee charged

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:24.420
somebody six thousand seven hundred fifty
dollars for three tests for coronavirus.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:28.350
You know, this this sort of thing has to be
talked about and illustrated and then in some

00:14:28.350 --> 00:14:32.510
way connected to people's lives
Speaker 1: in terms of the language that we're

