00:14:52.959 --> 00:14:53.990
Two part question.

00:14:53.990 --> 00:14:57.810
One, to what degree do you see the language
as analogous?

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:04.920
And two, are these politically astute comparisons
to be making from a practical perspective,

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:05.920
even if they are true?

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:08.200
Speaker 4: I think they're absolutely practical.

00:15:08.200 --> 00:15:12.830
And I know his work and I know him and I and
I think that if we can't learn from the past,

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:17.110
as the old saying goes, we're more than doomed
to repeat it.

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:20.950
We might we might find ourselves in places
that we never could have imagined could happen.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.630
We might find ourselves faced with the unthinkable.

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:26.890
And I think that when we talk about ultranationalism,
we talk about militarism.

00:15:26.890 --> 00:15:30.530
I mean, we talk about, for instance, the 1930s
in the 40s.

00:15:30.530 --> 00:15:36.050
One of the things that we have to make clear
is simply because factually what I call fascist

00:15:36.050 --> 00:15:42.300
politics emerges but doesn't present itself
in an exact replica does not mean it doesn't

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:43.300
